3... d6 4. Nc3 { Develops a piece. It is safe here because Black has not played ...Nf6, so ...Nxe4 is not possible. } 4... Nf6 ( 4... Be6 5. Bxe6 { Trades bishops so Black has to take back with the f-pawn, leaving doubled pawns on e6 and e5. } 5... fxe6 6. d4 { Opens the centre while Black's king is still on e8. } ) ( 4... h6 5. d4 { Takes the centre. Black spent a move on ...h6 instead of developing. } 5... exd4 6. Nxd4 Nf6 7. Nxc6 { Trades so that Black has to take back with the b-pawn and is left with doubled pawns on the c-file. Plan: Qe2, Be3 and castle long. } ) ( 4... Be7 5. d4 exd4 6. Nxd4 Nf6 7. O-O { Castles. White has more space and freer pieces. } ) ( 4... Bg4 5. h3 { Asks the bishop what it wants. If it takes on f3 White gets the bishop pair; if it retreats, there is a trap. } 5... Bxf3 6. Qxf3 ) 5. Ng5 { Aims at [f7](r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/4p1N1/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq -) again. Black's only good answer is ...d5, otherwise White wins material on f7. [%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] } 5... d5 ( 5... Be6 6. Bxe6 fxe6 7. Nxe6 { The knight takes a pawn and attacks the queen and the rook on f8 together. } 7... Qd7 8. Nxf8 ) 6. exd5 Na5 ( 6... Nxd5 7. Nxf7 { The sacrifice works because the bishop protects the knight, and Black's knight on d5 is in trouble. } 7... Kxf7 8. Bxd5+ ) 7. Bb5+ { The bishop leaves with check and keeps the extra pawn. } 7... c6 8. dxc6 bxc6 9. Ba4 { Retreats from the attack by the c6 pawn to a square where the bishop is safe. } 9... h6 10. Nf3 { Retreats before Black's h-pawn drives the knight off. Plan: castle, play d3 and Re1, and keep the extra pawn. } *
